till pays for a switching rate that departs from the person's ideal, and switching too often still costs less than switching too rarely. The new part is that how lenient someone is toward switching too often is now their own trait, drawn from a population. In the incumbent `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`, everyone shares one value for this. The claim is that people disagree about pairs of highly alternating sequences more than one shared value allows. Everything else in the model is unchanged.\n\n**How it was built:** I copied the incumbent's code and made one swap. The single shared leniency parameter is replaced by a per-person version: 400 person slots, each set as a population mean plus a population spread times a standard-normal draw (the same non-centred form the model already uses for its other per-person parameters).\n\n**Self-check result:**\n- The short test fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −3493.1, against the incumbent's −3500.3. That fit was only 100 draws on one chain, and its PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable (8.7% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5), so the comparison is a rough hint, not a result.\n- The test fit took 108 seconds. Admission stops any sampling run after 30 minutes, and the full fit is much longer, so speed is unconfirmed.\n\n**What it doesn't address:** the critique's one significant finding was that people differ in how often they press Left. It didn't survive the FDR correction (q = 0.128). The two live models that add a personal side bias each gained less than 1 nat over their base, so I targeted the alternation mechanism instead. This model does nothing about side bias.\n\nFiles are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_5/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`","ttft_ms":8812,"type":"result","duration_ms":414960,"uuid":"c07abafd-0d15-482a-a3b2-f91737fc14d2","ttft_stream_ms":5658,"time_to_request_ms":69,"first_content_frame_ms":5658,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
